== THE SIXTH STAGE

Now when they had got almost quite out of this wilderness, Faithful chanced to cast his eye back, and spotted one coming after them, and he knew him. Oh! said Faithful to his brother, who is coming over there? Then Christian looked, and said, It is my good friend Evangelist. Yes, and my good friend too, said Faithful, for it was he who set me on the way to the gate. Now Evangelist had come up to them, and greeted them thus.

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Peace be with you#super[+], dearly beloved, and peace be to your helpers.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Welcome, welcome, my good Evangelist: the sight of your face brings to my remembrance your old kindness and unwearied labors for my eternal good.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: And a thousand times welcome, said good Faithful; your company, O sweet Evangelist, how desirable it is to us poor pilgrims!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: Then said Evangelist, How has it fared with you, my friends, since the time of our last parting? What have you#super[+] met with, and how have you behaved yourselves?]

Then Christian and Faithful told him of all things that had happened to them on the way; and how, and with what difficulty, they had arrived at that place.

Right glad am I, said Evangelist, not that you#super[+] have met with trials, but that you have been victors, and because you have, notwithstanding many weaknesses, continued in the way to this very day.

I say, right glad am I of this thing, and that for my own sake and yours: I have sown, and you#super[+] have reaped; and the day is coming when “both he who sows and they who reap will rejoice together,” John 4:36; that is, if you hold out: “for in due time you#super[+] will reap a harvest if you do not give up.” Gal 6:9. The crown is before you#super[+], and it is an incorruptible one; “run in such a way as to obtain it.” 1 Cor 9:24–27. There are some who set out for this crown, and after they have gone far for it, another comes in and takes it from them: “hold fast, therefore, to what you have, so that no one will take your crown.” Rev 3:11. You are not yet out of the gunshot of the devil; “you#super[+] have not yet resisted to the point of shedding your blood, in your struggle against sin.” Let the kingdom be always before you#super[+], and believe steadfastly concerning the things that are invisible. Let nothing that is on this side of the other world get within you. And, above all, look well to your own hearts and to their lusts; for they are “deceitful above all things and desperately wicked.” Set your#super[+] faces like a flint; you have all power in heaven and earth on your side.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian thanked him for his exhortations; but told him also that they would have him speak further to them, for their help the rest of the way; and all the more because they well knew that he was a prophet, and could tell them of things that might happen to them, and also how they might resist and overcome them. To this request Faithful also consented. So Evangelist began as follows.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Evangelist]: My sons, you#super[+] have heard in the word of the truth of the Gospel that you must “endure many hardships to enter the kingdom of God”; and again, that “in town after town chains and afflictions await you”; and therefore you cannot expect that you should go long on your pilgrimage without them, in some sort or other. You#super[+] have found something of the truth of these testimonies upon you already, and more will immediately follow: for now, as you see, you are almost out of this wilderness, and therefore you will soon come into a town that you will shortly see before you; and in that town you will be hard beset with enemies, who will strain hard to kill you; and be sure that one or both of you must seal the testimony which you hold with blood; but “be faithful even unto death, and the King will give you a crown of life.” He who dies there, although his death will be unnatural, and his pain perhaps great, will yet have the better of his fellow; not only because he will have arrived at the Celestial City soonest, but because he will escape many miseries that the other will meet with in the rest of his journey. But when you#super[+] have come to the town, and find fulfilled what I have related here, then remember your friend, and acquit yourselves like men, and “entrust your souls to your faithful Creator and continue to do good.”]

Then I saw in my dream that when they had got out of the wilderness, they at once saw a town before them, and the name of that town is Vanity; and at the town there is a fair kept, called Vanity Fair. It is kept all the year long. It bears the name of Vanity Fair because the town where it is kept is lighter than vanity, Ps 62:9; and also because all that is sold there, or that comes there, is vanity; as is the saying of the wise, “Everything to come is vanity.” Eccl 11:8; see also 1:2-14; 2:11-17; Isa 40:17.

This fair is no newly erected business, but a thing of ancient standing. I will show you its origin.

Almost five thousand years ago there were pilgrims walking to the Celestial City, as these two honest persons are: and Beelzebub, Apollyon, and Legion, with their companions, perceiving by the path that the pilgrims made that their way to the city lay through this town of Vanity, contrived to set up a fair here; a fair in which all sorts of vanity should be sold, and that it should last all the year long. Therefore at this fair all such merchandise is sold as houses, lands, trades, places, honors, preferments, titles, countries, kingdoms, lusts, pleasures; and delights of all sorts, as harlots, wives, husbands, children, masters, servants, lives, blood, bodies, souls, silver, gold, pearls, precious stones, and what not.

And moreover, at this fair there are at all times to be seen jugglings, cheats, games, plays, fools, apes, knaves, and rogues, and that of every kind.

Here are to be seen, too, and that for nothing, thefts, murders, adulteries, false-swearers, and that of a blood-red color.

And as in other fairs of less importance there are the separate rows and streets under their proper names, where such and such wares are sold; so here, likewise, you have the proper places, rows, streets (namely, countries and kingdoms), where the wares of this fair are soonest to be found. Here is the Britain Row, the French Row, the Italian Row, the Spanish Row, the German Row, where various sorts of vanities are to be sold. But as in other fairs some one commodity is the chief of all the fair, so the ware of Rome and her merchandise is greatly promoted in this fair; only our English nation, with some others, has taken a dislike to it.

Now, as I said, the way to the Celestial City lies just through this town, where this lively fair is kept; and he who will go to the city, and yet not go through this town, “would have to leave this world.” 1 Cor 5:10. The Prince of princes himself, when here, went through this town to his own country, and that upon a fair-day too; yes, and, as I think, it was Beelzebub, the chief lord of this fair, who invited him to buy of his vanities; yes, he would have made him lord of the fair, if he would only have done him reverence as he went through the town. Yes, because he was such a person of honor, Beelzebub took him from street to street, and showed him all the kingdoms of the world in a little time, that he might, if possible, allure that blessed One to bargain for and buy some of his vanities; but he had no mind for the merchandise, and therefore left the town without laying out so much as one farthing upon these vanities. Matt 4:8, 9; Luke 4:5–7. This fair, therefore, is an ancient thing, of long standing, and a very great fair.

Now these pilgrims, as I said, must go through this fair. Well, so they did; but behold, even as they entered into the fair, all the people in the fair were moved, and the town itself, as it were, in a hubbub about them; and that for several reasons: for,

First, The Pilgrims were clothed with a kind of clothing that was different from the clothing of any who traded in that fair. The people of the fair, therefore, made a great gazing upon them: some said they were fools; 1 Cor 4:9, 10; some, they were madmen; and some, they were foreigners.

Secondly, And as they wondered at their apparel, so they did likewise at their speech; for few could understand what they said. They naturally spoke the language of Canaan; but those who kept the fair were the men of this world: so that from one end of the fair to the other, they seemed barbarians to each other. 1 Cor 2:7, 8.

Thirdly, But what puzzled the merchants not a little was that these pilgrims set very light by all their wares. They did not care so much as to look at them; and if they called upon them to buy, they would put their fingers in their ears, and cry, “Turn away my eyes from beholding vanity,” Ps 119:37, and look upward, signifying that their trade and traffic was in heaven. Phil 3:20, 21.

One chanced, mockingly, seeing the behavior of the men, to say to them, “What will you#super[+] buy?” But they, looking gravely at him, said, “We buy the truth.” Prov 23:23. At that the occasion was taken to despise the men all the more; some mocking, some taunting, some speaking reproachfully, and some calling upon others to strike them. At last things came to a hubbub and great stir in the fair, so that all order was confounded. Now word was brought at once to the great one of the fair, who quickly came down, and deputed some of his most trusted friends to take into examination those men about whom the fair was almost overturned. So the men were brought to examination; and those who sat upon them asked them where they came from, where they were going, and what they were doing there in such an unusual garb. The men told them that they were pilgrims and strangers in the world, and that they were going to their own country, which was the heavenly Jerusalem, Heb 11:13–16; and that they had given no occasion to the men of the town, nor yet to the merchants, to abuse them so, and to hinder them on their journey, except that, when one asked them what they would buy, they said they would buy the truth. But those who were appointed to examine them did not believe them to be anything other than lunatics and mad, or else such as came to put all things into confusion in the fair. Therefore they took them and beat them, and smeared them with dirt, and then put them into the cage, that they might be made a spectacle to all the men of the fair. There, therefore, they lay for some time, and were made the objects of any man’s sport, or malice, or revenge; the great one of the fair laughing all the while at all that befell them. But the men being patient, and “not repaying insult with insult, but with blessing,” and giving good words for bad, and kindness for injuries done, some men in the fair who were more observant and less prejudiced than the rest began to check and blame the baser sort for their continual abuses done by them to the men. They, therefore, in an angry manner let fly at them in turn, counting them as bad as the men in the cage, and telling them that they seemed to be their confederates, and should be made partakers of their misfortunes. The others replied that, for all they could see, the men were quiet and sober, and intended nobody any harm; and that there were many who traded in their fair who were more worthy to be put into the cage, yes, and the pillory too, than were the men they had abused. Thus, after various words had passed on both sides (the men behaving themselves all the while very wisely and soberly before them), they fell to some blows among themselves, and did harm to one another. Then these two poor men were brought before their examiners again, and were charged as being guilty of the late hubbub that had been in the fair. So they beat them pitifully, and hung irons upon them, and led them in chains up and down the fair, for an example and a terror to others, lest any should speak on their behalf, or join themselves to them. But Christian and Faithful behaved themselves yet more wisely, and received the disgrace and shame that was cast upon them with so much meekness and patience that it won to their side several of the men in the fair (though only a few in comparison with the rest). This put the other party into a yet greater rage, so that they resolved on the death of these two men. So they threatened that neither cage nor irons should serve their turn, but that they should die for the abuse they had done, and for deluding the men of the fair.

Then they were sent back to the cage again, until further order should be taken with them. So they put them in, and made their feet fast in the stocks.

Here, also, they called again to mind what they had heard from their faithful friend Evangelist, and were the more confirmed in their way and sufferings by what he had told them would happen to them. They also now comforted each other, that whoever’s lot it was to suffer, even he should have the best of it; therefore each man secretly wished that he might have that honor. But committing themselves to the all-wise disposal of Him who rules all things, with much content they remained in the condition in which they were, until they should be otherwise disposed of.

Then a convenient time being appointed, they brought them out to their trial, to bring about their condemnation. When the time had come, they were brought before their enemies and arraigned. The judge’s name was Lord Hate-good; their indictment was one and the same in substance, though varying somewhat in form; and its contents were this: “That they were enemies to, and disturbers of, the trade; that they had made commotions and divisions in the town, and had won a party to their own most dangerous opinions, in contempt of the law of their prince.”

Then Faithful began to answer, that he had only set himself against that which had set itself against Him who is higher than the highest. And, said he, as for disturbance, I make none, being myself a man of peace; the people who were won to us were won by seeing our truth and innocence, and they are only turned from the worse to the better. And as to the king you talk of, since he is Beelzebub, the enemy of our Lord, I defy him and all his angels.

Then a proclamation was made, that those who had anything to say for their lord the king against the prisoner at the bar should appear at once and give their evidence. So three witnesses came in, namely, Envy, Superstition, and Pickthank. They were then asked if they knew the prisoner at the bar, and what they had to say for their lord the king against him.

Then Envy stood forth, and said to this effect: My lord, I have known this man a long time, and will testify upon my oath before this honorable bench that he is—

#speech[#smallcaps[Judge]: Wait; give him his oath.]

So they swore him in. Then he said, My lord, this man, in spite of his plausible name, is one of the vilest men in our country; he respects neither prince nor people, law nor custom, but does all that he can to fill all men with certain disloyal notions of his, which he in general calls principles of faith and holiness. And in particular, I myself once heard him affirm that Christianity and the customs of our town of Vanity were diametrically opposed, and could not be reconciled. By that saying, my lord, he at once condemns not only all our praiseworthy doings, but us in the doing of them.

Then the judge said to him, Have you anything more to say?

#speech[#smallcaps[Envy]: My lord, I could say much more, only I would not be tedious to the court. Yet if need be, when the other gentlemen have given their evidence, rather than anything should be lacking that will dispatch him, I will enlarge my testimony against him. So he was told to stand aside.]

Then they called Superstition, and told him to look at the prisoner. They also asked what he could say for their lord the king against him. Then they swore him in; so he began.

#speech[#smallcaps[Superstition]: My lord, I have no great acquaintance with this man, nor do I desire to know him any further. However, this I know, that he is a very pestilent fellow, from some talk that I had with him the other day in this town; for then, talking with him, I heard him say that our religion was worthless, and such that a man could by no means please God by it. Your lordship knows very well what must necessarily follow from that saying of his, my lord, namely, that we always worship in vain, are still in our sins, and finally shall be damned; and this is what I have to say.]

Then Pickthank was sworn, and told to say what he knew on behalf of their lord the king against the prisoner at the bar.

#speech[#smallcaps[Pickthank]: My lord, and all you gentlemen, I have known this fellow a long time, and have heard him speak things that ought not to be spoken; for he has railed against our noble prince Beelzebub, and has spoken contemptuously of his honorable friends, whose names are the Lord Old Man, the Lord Carnal Delight, the Lord Luxurious, the Lord Desire of Vain Glory, my old Lord Lechery, Sir Having Greedy, with all the rest of our nobility; and he has said, moreover, that if all men were of his mind, if possible, there is not one of these noblemen who should exist any longer in this town. Besides, he has not been afraid to rail against you, my lord, who are now appointed to be his judge, calling you an ungodly villain, with many other such vilifying terms, with which he has bespattered most of the gentry of our town.]

When this Pickthank had told his tale, the judge directed his speech to the prisoner at the bar, saying, You renegade, heretic, and traitor, have you heard what these honest gentlemen have testified against you?

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: May I speak a few words in my own defense?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Judge]: Sirrah, sirrah, you deserve to live no longer, but to be slain immediately on the spot; yet, so that all men may see our gentleness toward you, let us hear what you, vile renegade, have to say.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: 1\. I say, then, in answer to what Mr. Envy has spoken, I never said anything but this: that whatever rule, or laws, or custom, or people were flat against the word of God are diametrically opposed to Christianity. If I have said anything wrong in this, convince me of my error, and I am ready here before you to make my recantation.]

2\. As to the second, namely, Mr. Superstition and his charge against me, I said only this, that in the worship of God a divine faith is required; but there can be no divine faith without a divine revelation of the will of God. Therefore, whatever is thrust into the worship of God that does not agree with divine revelation cannot be done except by a human faith; and that faith will not be profitable to eternal life.

3\. As to what Mr. Pickthank has said, I say (setting aside such terms as that I am said to rail, and the like) that the prince of this town, with all the rabble, his attendants, named by this gentleman, are more fit to exist in hell than in this town and country. And so the Lord have mercy upon me.

Then the judge called to the jury (who all this while stood by to hear and observe), Gentlemen of the jury, you#super[+] see this man about whom so great an uproar has been made in this town; you have also heard what these worthy gentlemen have testified against him; also, you have heard his reply and confession. It now lies in your breasts to hang him or save his life; but yet I think it fitting to instruct you in our law.

There was an act made in the days of Pharaoh the Great, servant to our prince, that, lest those of a contrary religion should multiply and grow too strong for him, their males should be thrown into the river. Exod 1:22. There was also an act made in the days of Nebuchadnezzar the Great, another of his servants, that whoever would not fall down and worship his golden image should be thrown into a fiery furnace. Dan 3:6. There was also an act made in the days of Darius, that whoever for a time called upon any god but him should be cast into the lions’ den. Dan 6:7. Now this rebel has broken the substance of these laws, not only in thought (which is not to be borne), but also in word and deed; which must therefore necessarily be intolerable.

For that of Pharaoh, his law was made upon a supposition, to prevent mischief, no crime being yet apparent; but here a crime is apparent. For the second and third, you#super[+] see he argues against our religion; and for the treason that he has already confessed, he deserves to die the death.

Then the jury went out, whose names were Mr. Blindman, Mr. No-good, Mr. Malice, Mr. Love-lust, Mr. Live-loose, Mr. Heady, Mr. High-mind, Mr. Enmity, Mr. Liar, Mr. Cruelty, Mr. Hate-light, and Mr. Implacable; and every one of them gave his private verdict against him among themselves, and afterward they unanimously concluded to bring him in guilty before the judge. And first among themselves, Mr. Blindman, the foreman, said, I see clearly that this man is a heretic. Then said Mr. No-good, Away with such a fellow from the earth. Yes, said Mr. Malice, for I hate the very looks of him. Then said Mr. Love-lust, I could never endure him. Nor I, said Mr. Live-loose, for he would always be condemning my way. Hang him, hang him, said Mr. Heady. A sorry wretch, said Mr. High-mind. My heart rises against him, said Mr. Enmity. He is a rogue, said Mr. Liar. Hanging is too good for him, said Mr. Cruelty. Let us dispatch him out of the way, said Mr. Hate-light. Then said Mr. Implacable, If I might have all the world given me, I could not be reconciled to him; therefore let us at once bring him in guilty of death.

And so they did; therefore he was at once condemned to be taken from the place where he was to the place he had come from, and there to be put to the most cruel death that could be invented.

They therefore brought him out, to do with him according to their law; and first they scourged him, then they beat him with their fists, then they lanced his flesh with knives; after that, they stoned him with stones, then pricked him with their swords; and last of all, they burned him to ashes at the stake. Thus Faithful came to his end.

Now I saw that there stood behind the multitude a chariot and a pair of horses waiting for Faithful, who (as soon as his adversaries had dispatched him) was taken up into it, and immediately was carried up through the clouds with the sound of a trumpet, the nearest way to the celestial gate. But as for Christian, he had some respite, and was remanded back to prison; so he remained there for a while. But he who overrules all things, having the power of their rage in his own hand, so brought it about that Christian for that time escaped them, and went his way.

And as he went, he sang, saying,

#quote[“Well, Faithful, thou hast faithfully profest \
Unto thy Lord, with whom thou shalt be blest, \
When faithless ones, with all their vain delights, \
Are crying out under their hellish plights: \
Sing, Faithful, sing, and let thy name survive; \
For though they killed thee, thou art yet alive.”]
